ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1031 "Properties of Transformed Square Root Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1033 1875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5ba25c95-b937-5921-95e0-7207aa7b83f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1031 "The Domain of a Transformed Radical Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1033 1600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6bcb4fdf-14db-5859-bc2c-c769917007f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114865 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e40b10cf-2e70-5bbd-9fd3-d58911e802ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115143 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115143 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of $g(x) = -2\\sqrt[6]{x+1}-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfa03caf-a95c-5ba3-aa94-94698481226d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115143 #edb/ref 1074 "The domain of $y = \\sqrt[6]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $g(x) =  -2\\sqrt[6]{x+1}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $x+1$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n{x+1} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = g(x)$ is $x \\in [-1, \\infty).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/7e/7eeaa091e1a1417efa5800abe3fd937c78957123c425c5a0d2ef239ca4994732.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115149 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115149 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of $f(x) = -4\\sqrt[9]{-x-5} +10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c380798-9b3a-5d9d-96dc-d858d737e577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115149 #edb/ref 1074 "The domain of $y = \\sqrt[9]{x}$ is\n$x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115314 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a4a8d54a-c034-5646-b804-0dc96f6c21d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115330 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d78b6269-6217-5e55-a551-aeb079c111cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1045 "The Domain of a Transformed Radical Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115428 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f21cee26-c706-5e01-b4f5-4680c602786d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115988 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of $n(x)=-2\\sqrt[4]{x-2}-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff97f1ab-e635-52ba-aeb0-0953318962bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115988 #edb/ref 1074 "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $n(x)=-2\\sqrt[4]{x-2}-1,$ we replace $x$ with $x-2$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\nx-2\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=n(x)$ is $x\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=n(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9d1df320fa9d2664365c6990df105456cfd6614209e1421224b1202b777f0cb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116231 #edb/ref 1061 30456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39dca9db-2697-5a19-88ba-4bf709eab73d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116232 #edb/ref 1061 30457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35f1ec51-e7b5-54bf-b6c9-9262124c42a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116254 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116254 #edb/ref 1061 32413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116254 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84378b6c-1015-5abf-b009-0b4084dc93cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116254 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116255 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116255 #edb/ref 1061 32416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116255 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "243ee844-2c59-5aff-9483-b018bbc24862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116255 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116535 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the domain of a radical function $f(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}$ depends on whether $n$ (called the **index**) is even or odd. \n\nIf $n$ is *odd*, then the domain of $f(x)$ is *always* $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$ For example, the domain of  $f(x)= \\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$ \n\nWe can see this in the graph below: the function $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}$ extends across all values of $x.$\n\n![](images/c6/c64950f8f253168743e8349e3f1c4f37b3bf3ad8196ccdbc7d982b7cf337e209.png)\n\nNow, suppose that we want to find the domain of a transformed radical function, such as\n\n$$\ng(x)= -\\sqrt[3]{-x+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty),$ and graph transformations will not change this interval. Therefore, we conclude that the domain of $g(x)$ is also $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116535 #edb/ref 1065 4862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116535 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f36b7baa-b008-5800-85a0-71c8a1713b24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116535 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116537 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's go back to considering $f(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}.$ \n\nIf $n$ is *even*, then the domain of $f(x)$ is *always* $x\\in [0, \\infty).$ For example, the domain of  $f(x)= \\sqrt[4]{x}\\,$ is $x\\in [0, \\infty).$ \n\nWe can see this in the graph below: the function $f(x) = \\sqrt[4]{x}$ starts at $x=0$ and extends forever to the right.\n\n![](images/eb/eb93d815dc2a981645ea5a99248cef8f8189d4990ed269b778d691a13960fc31.png)\n\nNotice how similar this looks to the square root function! All curves of the form $y=\\sqrt[n]{x}$ for even $n$ have this shape.\n\nNow let's consider the function \n\n$$\ng(x)= 3\\sqrt[4]{x+2} -2.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $g(x) = 3\\sqrt[4]{x+2} -2,$ we proceed in exactly the same way as with square roots: we set the expression under the radical sign to be greater than or equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2 \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = g(x)$ is $x \\in [-2, \\infty).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c0f840ea79edd03231b93156ce050b2af718b751b0f84da6b7c6dab9105e3df.png)\n\n   \n\nIn general, to find the domain of a radical function of the form $y = \\sqrt[n]{f(x)},$ where $n$ is an even integer, we need to solve the inequality $f(x) \\geq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116537 #edb/ref 1065 5156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116537 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b35a06bf-3ad8-5d8a-899e-a094e9728c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116537 #edb/ref 1067 "The Domain of a Radical Function With an Even Index" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557878 #edb/ref 1080 "\\infty" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557878 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557878 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04c79d26-2ab5-5105-b377-4dfcbcc76684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557936 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557936 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557936 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20830856-dd64-505e-9bac-b19cd9da9f46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557968 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in [-8,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557968 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557968 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31648ea3-bede-5f95-a63a-3b889794c336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558014 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558014 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558014 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ddb34ce-cd00-5218-a021-318ddf3a6cc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558156 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in [1,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558156 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558156 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93dafbd0-e49a-51ea-9bb0-afc4c0d9f013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558169 #edb/ref 1080 "\\infty" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558169 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558169 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "